1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:01,169
Lot thirty-one!

2
00:00:01,209 --> 00:00:08,000
One upright parlor piano, property of the late
Samuel Whitmore, sold by order of the court!

3
00:00:08,730 --> 00:00:12,010
Mama... are they selling Grandma's piano?

4
00:00:12,683 --> 00:00:17,263
Hold my hand, Lily. Just hold my hand.

5
00:00:18,803 --> 00:00:25,783
A dirt farmer's widow with a parlor piano.
Pride goeth before the fall, ladies.

6
00:00:26,443 --> 00:00:31,849
Let's be generous, Deke.
Start it at five dollars.

7
00:00:32,363 --> 00:00:37,423
Six dollars! It will make lovely kindling.

8
00:00:38,702 --> 00:00:42,562
Six dollars, going once... going twice...

9
00:00:42,988 --> 00:00:45,988
Two hundred.

10
00:00:46,625 --> 00:00:49,005
Two hun... two hundred... dollars, mister?

11
00:00:49,375 --> 00:00:50,925
In gold.

12
00:00:51,875 --> 00:00:54,425
...Sold.

13
00:00:55,242 --> 00:00:59,242
Deliver it to the Whitmore place. Tonight.

14
00:01:10,983 --> 00:01:13,850
Cedar Bluff, Colorado Territory.

15
00:01:14,373 --> 00:01:17,066
October, 1879.

16
00:01:18,170 --> 00:01:22,871
Until that morning, Clara Whitmore had
not cried in front of a single soul.

17
00:01:23,301 --> 00:01:25,111
Not at her husband's funeral.

18
00:01:25,542 --> 00:01:28,269
Not when the letters from
the bank began to arrive.

19
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:32,042
And not when the sheriff nailed
the court's notice to her door.

20
00:01:32,589 --> 00:01:38,323
If this story finds its way to your
heart, stay with Clara until the very end,

21
00:01:38,452 --> 00:01:42,004
and tell me in the comments where
you are watching from tonight.

22
00:01:43,154 --> 00:01:45,696
Samuel Whitmore had been a good man.

23
00:01:45,929 --> 00:01:49,400
He came home from the war with
a scar he never talked about,

24
00:01:49,691 --> 00:01:52,151
a Union coat he never wore again,

25
00:01:52,373 --> 00:01:57,074
and a dream of a hundred and sixty
acres under the Colorado sky.

26
00:01:57,353 --> 00:02:02,484
For seven years, he and Clara built
that dream with their own hands.

27
00:02:02,717 --> 00:02:04,713
A cabin of pine logs.

28
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:08,080
A barn. A fence of split rails.

29
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:10,890
And a little girl named Lily.

30
00:02:11,425 --> 00:02:13,792
Then came the summer of the great drought.

31
00:02:14,025 --> 00:02:16,068
The creek dried to a trickle.

32
00:02:16,301 --> 00:02:18,424
The corn burned in the field.

33
00:02:18,878 --> 00:02:20,979
And for the first time in his life,

34
00:02:21,072 --> 00:02:25,553
Sam Whitmore walked into the Cedar
Bluff Bank with his hat in his hand.

35
00:02:25,844 --> 00:02:28,073
Horace Pike was happy to help.

36
00:02:28,190 --> 00:02:30,662
He was always happy to help.

37
00:02:31,011 --> 00:02:35,306
Three hundred dollars, for
seed and a winter's flour.

38
00:02:35,852 --> 00:02:38,858
Sam signed the note without
reading the small print.

39
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:41,517
Men like Sam never did.

40
00:02:41,866 --> 00:02:44,930
That winter, a fever
came through the valley.

41
00:02:45,291 --> 00:02:47,345
It took four men in Cedar Bluff.

42
00:02:47,787 --> 00:02:49,551
One of them was Sam.

43
00:02:50,086 --> 00:02:52,751
Clara buried him on the
hill behind the house,

44
00:02:52,791 --> 00:02:54,845
under the aspens he had planted.

45
00:02:55,194 --> 00:02:58,828
And then she did what women on
the frontier had always done.

46
00:02:59,189 --> 00:03:00,337
She kept going.

47
00:03:00,628 --> 00:03:02,032
She sold eggs.

48
00:03:02,207 --> 00:03:03,599
She took in mending.

49
00:03:04,053 --> 00:03:08,715
And three afternoons a week, the children
of Cedar Bluff came to her parlor to

50
00:03:08,755 --> 00:03:10,066
learn the piano.

51
00:03:10,357 --> 00:03:15,976
Her mother's piano, carried across
the plains in a wagon in 1858.

52
00:03:16,523 --> 00:03:19,030
The only beautiful thing she owned.

53
00:03:19,448 --> 00:03:23,523
But Horace Pike's interest
grew faster than any corn.

54
00:03:24,139 --> 00:03:26,518
Three hundred became four hundred.

55
00:03:27,018 --> 00:03:29,943
Four hundred became
four hundred and eighty.

56
00:03:30,362 --> 00:03:32,183
With penalties on top.

57
00:03:32,683 --> 00:03:36,839
Clara was not the first widow in
the valley to owe Horace Pike money.

58
00:03:37,385 --> 00:03:40,902
The Hendersons had lost their
place the spring before.

59
00:03:41,356 --> 00:03:45,035
The Kowalski farm, the autumn before that.

60
00:03:45,489 --> 00:03:50,097
And each time, Pike bought
the land himself, at auction,

61
00:03:50,272 --> 00:03:51,897
for whatever he pleased.

62
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:57,400
So when the court ordered her household
goods sold to pay the arrears,

63
00:03:57,692 --> 00:04:01,174
Clara understood exactly what Pike wanted.

64
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:03,542
He didn't want her piano.

65
00:04:04,089 --> 00:04:07,838
He wanted her to know
she had nothing left.

66
00:04:08,733 --> 00:04:12,912
That evening, the parlor felt
like a church after a funeral.

67
00:04:13,365 --> 00:04:17,923
There was a pale square on the floorboards
where the piano had stood for six

68
00:04:17,963 --> 00:04:24,297
years. Clara swept around it twice
before she understood that she was afraid

69
00:04:24,337 --> 00:04:25,427
to step on it.

70
00:04:25,974 --> 00:04:29,073
Lily fell asleep without
asking for a song.

71
00:04:29,527 --> 00:04:33,461
And Clara sat by the window
until the lamp burned low,

72
00:04:33,636 --> 00:04:35,969
wondering who the stranger was,

73
00:04:36,144 --> 00:04:41,345
and what a man like that could
possibly want with a widow's piano.

74
00:04:42,545 --> 00:04:47,045
That's far enough. If you've come to collect,
mister, there's nothing left to sell.

75
00:04:48,407 --> 00:04:52,027
I didn't come to collect, ma'am.
I came to return something.

76
00:04:53,085 --> 00:04:58,391
I don't take charity. Not from
the bank, and not from strangers.

77
00:04:59,065 --> 00:05:06,375
Then don't call it charity. Your east fence
is down. I'll fix it for a hot supper.

78
00:05:07,565 --> 00:05:10,765
One supper. And you sleep in the barn.

79
00:05:11,735 --> 00:05:12,735
Yes, ma'am.

80
00:05:14,025 --> 00:05:15,858
One supper became two.

81
00:05:16,115 --> 00:05:17,600
Two became a week.

82
00:05:18,088 --> 00:05:22,975
The stranger gave his name only as
Mercer, and he gave little else.

83
00:05:23,290 --> 00:05:25,204
He was up before the rooster.

84
00:05:25,612 --> 00:05:29,465
He rebuilt the east fence,
rehung the barn door,

85
00:05:29,547 --> 00:05:32,542
and split enough wood
to last until Christmas.

86
00:05:32,996 --> 00:05:37,314
He never asked for wages,
and he never asked questions.

87
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:41,052
Lily followed him everywhere
like a small shadow.

88
00:05:41,506 --> 00:05:44,930
He showed her how to brush
a horse in long strokes,

89
00:05:45,105 --> 00:05:46,811
the way the hair grows,

90
00:05:46,870 --> 00:05:50,422
and how to talk to an animal
so it learns to trust you.

91
00:05:51,363 --> 00:05:55,925
Clara watched from the kitchen window
and told herself not to get used to it.

92
00:05:56,274 --> 00:05:59,710
At supper, the three of
them ate mostly in silence.

93
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:03,018
It was not an unkind silence.

94
00:06:03,298 --> 00:06:06,502
And at night, for the
first time since Sam died,

95
00:06:06,677 --> 00:06:08,313
Clara played again.

96
00:06:08,755 --> 00:06:10,716
She played the old songs.

97
00:06:11,170 --> 00:06:15,395
And always, at the very
end, she played Shenandoah.

98
00:06:15,942 --> 00:06:18,715
The song Sam used to
whistle while he worked.

99
00:06:19,169 --> 00:06:23,452
She did not know that out on
the porch step, in the dark,

100
00:06:23,511 --> 00:06:27,481
the stranger sat with his hat in
his hands and his eyes closed,

101
00:06:27,575 --> 00:06:30,105
listening to every note.

102
00:06:31,555 --> 00:06:33,555
My husband used to whistle that song.

103
00:06:34,267 --> 00:06:38,007
Lot of men did. Back then.

104
00:06:38,775 --> 00:06:41,711
On the fourth morning,
a calf went missing,

105
00:06:41,886 --> 00:06:45,020
and Mercer rode up to the
north ridge to look for it.

106
00:06:45,474 --> 00:06:46,820
He found the calf.

107
00:06:47,111 --> 00:06:49,061
He also found something else.

108
00:06:49,514 --> 00:06:53,542
Wooden stakes, driven into
the rock in a straight line,

109
00:06:53,671 --> 00:06:56,921
each with a strip of red
cloth snapping in the wind.

110
00:06:57,235 --> 00:06:58,964
Surveyor's stakes.

111
00:06:59,278 --> 00:07:00,462
Fresh ones.

112
00:07:00,869 --> 00:07:03,527
The rock around them had
been chipped and split,

113
00:07:03,736 --> 00:07:07,509
and in the morning sun,
the broken stone glittered.

114
00:07:07,997 --> 00:07:14,022
And there, in the dust beside the stakes,
lay the stub of an expensive cigar,

115
00:07:14,313 --> 00:07:16,866
still wrapped in its gold paper band.

116
00:07:17,529 --> 00:07:22,811
There was only one man in Cedar
Bluff who smoked cigars like that.

117
00:07:24,003 --> 00:07:28,063
Silver. So that's what you're after.

118
00:07:29,337 --> 00:07:34,117
Mrs. Whitmore. I've come with a kindness.

119
00:07:34,858 --> 00:07:40,675
Five hundred for the land. Clears
your debt and buys you a ticket east.

120
00:07:42,445 --> 00:07:43,605
It's not for sale.

121
00:07:45,048 --> 00:07:46,488
And who might you be?

122
00:07:47,762 --> 00:07:49,542
A man who pays his debts.

123
00:07:50,325 --> 00:07:57,875
The note comes due Saturday, Mrs. Whitmore.
The court doesn't care about pianos.

124
00:07:58,275 --> 00:08:02,454
That night, Clara counted everything
she had on the kitchen table.

125
00:08:02,896 --> 00:08:03,777
Egg money.

126
00:08:04,138 --> 00:08:05,194
Lesson money.

127
00:08:05,740 --> 00:08:08,050
The silver locket her mother had left her.

128
00:08:08,596 --> 00:08:10,244
Sixty-three dollars.

129
00:08:10,791 --> 00:08:13,483
The stranger's gold had paid the arrears.

130
00:08:13,844 --> 00:08:17,627
But the note itself, four
hundred and eighty dollars,

131
00:08:17,721 --> 00:08:19,601
came due on Saturday.

132
00:08:20,148 --> 00:08:23,126
If it wasn't paid, the
court would sell the land,

133
00:08:23,166 --> 00:08:25,534
the house, and everything in it.

134
00:08:25,895 --> 00:08:31,908
And everyone in Cedar Bluff knew
who would be the only bidder.

135
00:08:32,817 --> 00:08:37,530
Somewhere past midnight, the horses in
the corral began to stamp and snort.

136
00:08:37,983 --> 00:08:41,663
Three riders came down off the
ridge without a light between them.

137
00:08:42,024 --> 00:08:45,599
They only lit their torches
when they reached the barn.

138
00:08:52,125 --> 00:08:54,815
The next one goes lower!

139
00:08:56,277 --> 00:08:58,357
Tell Pike the lady isn't selling.

140
00:09:07,738 --> 00:09:10,698
Why are you doing this?
You don't even know us.

141
00:09:11,692 --> 00:09:12,872
Ask me Saturday.

142
00:09:13,942 --> 00:09:17,703
When the sun came up, the
barn wall was black with soot,

143
00:09:17,936 --> 00:09:19,665
but it was still standing.

144
00:09:20,026 --> 00:09:23,345
And by the next morning,
the stranger was gone.

145
00:09:23,892 --> 00:09:26,155
His horse was gone from the stall.

146
00:09:26,608 --> 00:09:28,941
His bedroll was gone from the hay.

147
00:09:29,395 --> 00:09:30,683
There was no note.

148
00:09:30,974 --> 00:09:33,802
Only a mended fence, a full woodpile,

149
00:09:33,842 --> 00:09:36,941
and a little girl standing at the window.

150
00:09:38,168 --> 00:09:40,808
He'll come back, Mama. He said Saturday.

151
00:09:41,677 --> 00:09:43,897
Men say a lot of things, sweetheart.

152
00:09:44,650 --> 00:09:47,598
Clara told herself she had expected it.

153
00:09:48,052 --> 00:09:51,534
Men left. That was the way of the world.

154
00:09:51,988 --> 00:09:56,503
The war had taught her that, and
the fever had taught her again.

155
00:09:57,049 --> 00:10:02,424
On Thursday, Pike's man rode out and
nailed a new notice to her gatepost.

156
00:10:02,971 --> 00:10:06,720
The Whitmore homestead, to
be sold at public auction,

157
00:10:06,953 --> 00:10:08,531
Saturday at noon.

158
00:10:09,194 --> 00:10:13,605
On Friday, Clara took Sam's old
Union coat out of the trunk,

159
00:10:13,698 --> 00:10:17,250
folded it, and laid it on
top of her few dresses.

160
00:10:17,704 --> 00:10:21,019
And that night, she closed
the lid of her mother's piano,

161
00:10:21,059 --> 00:10:22,974
and did not play.

162
00:10:23,988 --> 00:10:27,464
The Whitmore homestead,
one hundred sixty acres!

163
00:10:27,504 --> 00:10:28,928
Opening bid from Mr.

164
00:10:28,968 --> 00:10:31,458
Pike: four hundred eighty dollars!

165
00:10:31,528 --> 00:10:37,474
Well. Now she'll finally learn her place.

166
00:10:43,868 --> 00:10:49,474
Before anyone bids, the marshal
has a question for Mr. Pike.

167
00:10:49,868 --> 00:10:57,458
Horace Pike. You filed a silver claim in
Denver on land you don't own. That's fraud.

168
00:10:57,848 --> 00:10:59,808
Preposterous!

169
00:11:00,437 --> 00:11:05,777
Four hundred eighty. The
Whitmore note is paid. In full.

170
00:11:19,195 --> 00:11:22,295
It's Saturday. Why?

171
00:11:23,495 --> 00:11:23,995
Shiloh.

172
00:11:23,993 --> 00:11:25,374
April of 'sixty-two.

173
00:11:25,414 --> 00:11:29,395
Your husband carried me two
miles with a ball in my leg.

174
00:11:30,442 --> 00:11:31,680
He made me promise.

175
00:11:31,720 --> 00:11:36,162
If I ever got west, his Clara would
never sell her mother's piano.

176
00:11:36,900 --> 00:11:43,122
The letter was soft as cloth from being
folded and unfolded a thousand times.

177
00:11:43,564 --> 00:11:45,839
The handwriting was Sam's.

178
00:11:46,130 --> 00:11:50,176
John Mercer had been a twenty-two-year-old
private when the line broke at

179
00:11:50,216 --> 00:11:53,849
Shiloh. A musket ball shattered his leg.

180
00:11:54,140 --> 00:11:58,640
He would have died in the mud if a
sergeant from Ohio had not thrown him over

181
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:02,243
his shoulders and carried him
two miles to the surgeons' tents,

182
00:12:02,418 --> 00:12:04,995
whistling Shenandoah the whole way,

183
00:12:05,228 --> 00:12:08,258
so the boy would not
hear his own screaming.

184
00:12:08,804 --> 00:12:11,764
After the war, Sam wrote to him once.

185
00:12:11,939 --> 00:12:13,192
Just once.

186
00:12:13,367 --> 00:12:14,899
One promise.

187
00:12:15,352 --> 00:12:20,049
It took Mercer fifteen years and a cattle
business in Wyoming before he could

188
00:12:20,089 --> 00:12:23,455
keep it. By the time he found Cedar Bluff,

189
00:12:23,549 --> 00:12:26,021
Sam had been in the ground for a year.

190
00:12:26,475 --> 00:12:28,877
He had come too late for his friend.

191
00:12:29,238 --> 00:12:32,314
But he had not come too late for her.

192
00:12:33,233 --> 00:12:37,307
Horace Pike was taken to Denver
in irons that same afternoon.

193
00:12:37,854 --> 00:12:41,707
Before the month was out, the
Hendersons had their deed back.

194
00:12:42,161 --> 00:12:46,235
And that evening, as the sun
went down behind the aspens,

195
00:12:46,410 --> 00:12:49,637
the windows of the
Whitmore house glowed gold,

196
00:12:49,870 --> 00:12:54,741
and the sound of a piano drifted out
over the valley for the first time in a

197
00:12:54,781 --> 00:12:58,031
week. Clara played Shenandoah.

198
00:12:58,392 --> 00:13:00,655
Lily sat beside her on the bench.

199
00:13:00,888 --> 00:13:05,229
And in the doorway, a tall man
stood with his hat in his hands,

200
00:13:05,288 --> 00:13:07,934
waiting to be asked in.

201
00:13:08,868 --> 00:13:13,768
Supper's at six, Mr. Mercer.
Every night, if you'd like.

202
00:13:14,775 --> 00:13:16,015
I'd like that, ma'am.

203
00:13:23,025 --> 00:13:27,204
Thank you for riding along with
Clara, Lily, and Mercer tonight.

204
00:13:27,657 --> 00:13:29,735
If this story warmed your heart,

205
00:13:29,828 --> 00:13:32,881
there are more stories waiting
for you on this channel.

206
00:13:33,335 --> 00:13:37,293
Until next time in Cedar Bluff,
keep a light in the window.

